 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed board changes are pushed to browsers over self-hosted WebSockets rather than five-second HTTPS polling or a hosted realtime service. Five-second polling keeps delivery stateless but incurs repeated reads and multi-second lag; a hosted realtime service reduces connection operations but introduces recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted WebSockets deliver fast updates without vendor lock-in or recurring realtime fees, accepting ownership of connection capacity, session management, and reconnect/catch-up logic across change storage, delivery, and browser sync. See [docs/decisions/change-delivery.md](decisions/change-delivery.md).
